---
import ReferenceLayout from '@layouts/ReferenceLayout.astro';
import { getReference } from '@data/reference';

const ref = getReference('ideal-weight-table')!;
---

<ReferenceLayout reference={ref}>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    Ideal body weight for each height, for men and women. Because the classic formulas disagree, each value
    is shown as a range across all four — treat it as a healthy target zone, not a single number.
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="howto">
    <h2>How to use this chart</h2>
    <p>
      Find your height and read the range for your sex. It spans the Robinson, Miller, Devine and Hamwi
      formulas, which were each derived differently — so a healthy target usually sits somewhere within the
      range. Frame size, muscle and age all shift what is right for an individual.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="notes">
    <h2>Methodology</h2>
    <p>
      Each range is the lowest and highest of the Robinson, Miller, Devine and Hamwi ideal-body-weight formulas
      for the given height and sex, computed with this site's unit-tested function and rounded to the nearest
      pound. These formulas were originally developed for clinical use; a healthy weight is better thought of as
      a range. For your figures and the healthy-BMI range, use the <a href={`/${ref.relatedCalculator}`}>ideal weight calculator</a>.
      This is general information, not medical advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ReferenceLayout>
